Certainly! Below is a TikZ code that replicates the image you described. This example will create a simple diagram with nodes and edges.

```latex
\documentclass{standalone}
\usepackage{tikz}

\begin{document}

\begin{tikzpicture}[node distance=2cm, auto]
    \node (A) {A};
    \node (B) [right of=A] {B};
    \node (C) [below of=B] {C};
    \node (D) [above of=C] {D};

    \draw[->] (A) -- (B);
    \draw[->] (B) -- (C);
    \draw[->] (C) -- (D);
    \draw[->] (D) -- (A);
\end{tikzpicture}

\end{document}
```

This code creates a simple directed graph with four nodes labeled A, B, C, and D. The arrows indicate the direction of the edges between the nodes. You can compile this code using a LaTeX editor or online LaTeX compiler to see the result.

